re looking to defend their crown in Amsterdam. They sent a new crew, consisting of " [:a {:href "/athletes/39701"} "Hans Bourquin"] ", at age 13 the youngest rowing competitor in the tournament, and brothers " [:a {:href "/athletes/39809"} "Hans"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39808"} "Karl Schöchlin"] ", who had been European champions in the double sculls and eights in 1922 and 1925, respectively."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the opening round, however, France’s trio of " [:a {:href "/athletes/37075"} "Henri Préaux"] " and brothers " [:a {:href "/athletes/37032"} "Armand"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37033"} "Édouard Marcelle"] " emerged as contenders, and the Swiss were able to eke out a victory by only two-tenths of a second. The crew from the Netherlands, meanwhile, capsized during their race against Belgium and had to be rescued. With France defeating the Americans in the repêchage, the stage was set for a Switzerland-France rematch in the final, as these two nations were clearly superior to the rest of the field. Italy dropped out in the quarterfinals, however, essentially giving the Swiss team a walkover in the semifinals and allowing them to conserve their strength, while the French crew were forced to face the Belgians for a second time. Thus, the final was more decisive, with an exhausted French trio unable to keep up with the Swiss, who captured the gold medal. Belgium took home bronze by default, having no one to compete against in a third-place race."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "Switzerland"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "8"} [:a